# recipe.gustale.com (SINGULAR) — the common typo for recipes.gustale.com
#
# Added 2026-11-12. A 301 to the recipes site.
#
# THE DNS SITUATION (read this before changing anything)
# ----------------------------------------------------
# At the time of writing, recipe.gustale.com has an A record pointing at
# 15.197.162.184 -- NOT at this VPS (62.72.7.218). That address is a
# registrar's URL-redirect service, which already answers with:
#
#     HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently
#     Location: https://recipes.gustale.com
#
# So the singular host already redirects, and it did so before any of
# this. What the registrar's redirect does NOT do is preserve the path:
#
#     recipe.gustale.com/dishes/bakso/   ->  https://recipes.gustale.com/
#     recipe.gustale.com/families        ->  https://recipes.gustale.com/
#
# Every deep link collapses onto the homepage.
#
# THAT IS CONFIRMED, NOT ASSUMED. Measured on 2026-11-16:
#
#     recipe.gustale.com/                     -> 301  https://recipes.gustale.com/
#     recipe.gustale.com/recipes/moussaka-greek -> 301  https://recipes.gustale.com/
#     recipe.gustale.com/dishes/moussaka-greek  -> 301  https://recipes.gustale.com/
#
# The path is gone in all three.
#
# THE PLAN
# --------
# 1. The owner changes the A record for recipe.gustale.com to
#    62.72.7.218. Until that happens this block is dormant -- Caddy
#    never sees the host.
# 2. Caddy picks up the host, obtains a certificate for it, and serves
#    the 301 itself -- WITH the path preserved.
# 3. At the single-domain cutover the target changes from
#    recipes.gustale.com to gustale.com, which is the canonical site.
#
# WHY SERVE IT OURSELVES RATHER THAN LEAVING IT AT THE REGISTRAR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Two reasons, one of which only matters after the cutover.
#
# - The path. A registrar redirect drops it. A shared link of the form
#   recipe.gustale.com/dishes/bakso/ should land on the dish, not the
#   homepage. Links get shortened and pasted, and people type the
#   singular form.
# - Control. The registrar's redirect target is set in the registrar's
#   panel. At cutover the canonical host changes. If the
#   redirect lives in the registrar it has to be remembered, updated,
#   and verified separately from the deploy -- a second place for the
#   cutover to go wrong, at exactly the moment when the redirect
#   matters most.
#
# PATH PRESERVATION
# -----------------
# `redir` rewrites the host and keeps the path AND the query string:
#
#     recipe.gustale.com/dishes/bakso/   ->  https://gustale.com/dishes/bakso/
#     recipe.gustale.com/families?q=indonesia -> https://gustale.com/families?q=indonesia
#
# A CORRECTION TO THE COMMENT THAT WAS HERE BEFORE
# ------------------------------------------------
# The previous version of this file said:
#
#     "The explicit `{uri}` is not needed and would drop the query
#      string."
#
# That is BACKWARDS, and it is worth recording why, because a
# plausible-sounding wrong comment is worse than no comment.
#
# `{uri}` expands to `http.request.uri`, which is Go's
# `req.URL.RequestURI()` -- the path AND the query string. It is
# `{path}` that drops the query.
#
# Confirmed from the Caddy source, not from memory:
#
#     caddyconfig/httpcaddyfile/shorthands.go
#         "uri"   -> http.request.uri
#         "path"  -> http.request.uri.path
#     modules/caddyhttp/replacer.go
#         http.request.uri -> req.URL.RequestURI()   // path + query
#
# So `{uri}` is exactly what this rule needs: it carries the path
# across AND keeps the query string, in one placeholder.
#
# THE TARGET
# ----------
# gustale.com. It does not redirect, so this is ONE hop. The old
# target, recipes.gustale.com, becomes a redirect host itself after
# the cutover -- pointing this rule at it would make a TWO-HOP chain,
# and a two-hop chain is slow, breaks cached links, and leaks the
# referrer through the intermediate.
#
# Note the special case: the bare root "/" would become
# "https://gustale.com/" by the `{uri}` rule, but the root of the
# recipes site redirects to "https://gustale.com/recipes" -- so the
# root gets its own rule, first.
#
# THE RULES ARE THE SAME THREE THAT THE OTHER REDIRECT HOSTS USE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# See gustale.recipes.caddy for the full explanation of each rule and
# for the three Caddy behaviours that are easy to get wrong.
# scripts/verify-redirect-rules.sh exercises this file against a real
# Caddy alongside the other two.

recipe.gustale.com {
	# The root of the recipes site is the recipes HUB, not the
	# site home. Declared and used FIRST, because the catch-all
	# below would otherwise send it to the site home.
	@recipes_root path /
	handle @recipes_root {
		redir https://gustale.com/recipes{?query} permanent
	}

	# EVERYTHING ELSE -> the same path on gustale.com.
	#
	# `{uri}` is the path AND the query string.
	handle {
		redir https://gustale.com{uri} permanent
	}

	# The redirect responses themselves carry no cache. A cached 301
	# is a 301 that cannot be fixed without a cache bust.
	header {
		Cache-Control "no-store"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}

	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/recipe.gustale.com.log {
			roll_size 100mb
			roll_keep 5
		}
	}
}
